/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - External noise enters through the antenna.
   - Internal noise is made inside the receiver's own components.
   - P is the available noise power in watts, delivered to a matched load; k = 1.38×10−23 J/K is Boltzmann's constant; T is in kelvin; B is the noise bandwidth in hertz; R is the resistance in ohms; vn is the RMS open-circuit noise voltage.
   - Resistors in series add their mean-square voltages, because their noises are independent: vn² = 4kT(R1 + R2)B.
   - White noise has the same power spectral density (PSD) at every frequency, as white light holds every colour.
   - Its total power would be infinite, so white noise is a model, valid over the band the receiver actually uses (thermal noise at room temperature stays nearly flat up to about 1 THz).
   - A real filter has sloping skirts.
   - Thermal noise is the sum of an enormous number of independent electron motions, so by the central limit theorem it is Gaussian with zero mean.
   - After the receiver's band-pass filter the noise is narrowband noise, centred on fc with bandwidth B.
   - AWGN joins the pieces: the noise adds to the signal, r(t) = s(t) + n(t); its amplitude is Gaussian; its PSD is flat.
*/
