/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Feed an amplifier of power gain G from a matched source at the standard temperature T0 = 290 K.
   - F is a linear ratio with no unit; the second form follows because the signal is amplified by G as well; F = 1 + Na/(G kT0B).
   - F is at least 1, and a noiseless device has F = 1.
   - The equivalent noise temperature Te describes the added noise as if it came from an extra matched resistor at the input: the added noise power, referred to the input, is kTeB, and at the output it is G kTeB.
   - Te and T0 = 290 K are in kelvin; F is linear.
   - F = 2 means Te = 290 K.
   - A lossy cable or attenuator with power loss L (input power over output power, L ≥ 1) has gain G = 1/L.
   - A receiver is a chain of stages with linear gains G1, G2, … and noise factors F1, F2, ….
   - F = F1 + (F2 − 1)/G1 + (F3 − 1)/(G1G2) + …
   - Te = Te1 + Te2/G1 + Te3/(G1G2) + …
*/
