/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 1 MHz receiver chain has an antenna of temperature Ta = 50 K, a cable with 2 dB loss at 290 K, an LNA (G = 20 dB, NF = 1 dB) and a mixer (G = 10 dB, NF = 8 dB). Find Te, NF, Tsys and the input noise power (a) with the cable before the LNA, and (b) with the LNA moved to the antenna and the cable after it.
   1. Cable: L = 100.2 = 1.585, G = 0.631, Te = 0.585 × 290 = 169.6 K. LNA: F = 100.1 = 1.259, Te = 75.1 K, G = 100. Mixer: F = 100.8 = 6.310, Te = 1539.8 K.
   2. (a) Cable first: Te = 169.6 + 75.1/0.631 + 1539.8/(0.631 × 100) = 169.6 + 119.0 + 24.4 = 313.0 K, so F = 1 + 313.0/290 = 2.079 and NF = 3.18 dB.
   3. (b) LNA first: Te = 75.1 + 169.6/100 + 1539.8/(100 × 0.631) = 75.1 + 1.7 + 24.4 = 101.2 K, so F = 1.349 and NF = 1.30 dB.
   4. Tsys = Ta + Te: (a) 50 + 313.0 = 363.0 K; (b) 50 + 101.2 = 151.2 K.
   5. Noise power kTsysB with B = 106 Hz: (a) 1.38×10−23 × 363.0 × 106 = 5.01×10−15 W (−113.0 dBm); (b) 2.09×10−15 W (−116.8 dBm).
   6. Moving the LNA ahead of the cable lowers the noise by 10 log10(363.0/151.2) = 3.8 dB, so the same signal arrives with 3.8 dB more SNR. In (a) the cable's 2 dB loss also divides the LNA's term by 0.631, raising it from 75.1 K to 119.0 K.
   Answer: (a) Te = 313.0 K, NF = 3.18 dB, Tsys = 363.0 K, −113.0 dBm. (b) Te = 101.2 K, NF = 1.30 dB, Tsys = 151.2 K, −116.8 dBm: 3.8 dB better. */
