/* book exam traps: Do not add noise figures in dB, and do not put dB values into Friis. Convert to linear F and G, apply the formula, then convert back. | T0 is 290 K, not 300 K. Using 300 K shifts every Te and is a common marking error. | F is linear and NF is in dB. F cannot be below 1, so NF cannot be negative. | A lossy cable at T0 has F = L and gain 1/L. Before the LNA it adds its own noise and also multiplies the later stages' terms by L. | The last stage's gain never appears in Friis. And Te is referred to the input: at the output the added noise is G kTeB. */
